      "id": 1,
      "text": "Perezville was once called San Diego, renamed in 1852 when the first post office opened in a blockhouse on the west bank of San Diego Creek.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.texasalmanac.com/places/perezville",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Perezville is a colonia located on State Spur 347, eight miles west of Mission in southwestern Hidalgo County.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.tshaonline.org/handbook/entries/perezville-tx",
      "category": "other",
      "quality": "poor",
      "qualityReason": "location_description"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Early settlers were Julián Flores' herdsmen who arrived by 1815, and the town was founded around 1819 as San Diego.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        16
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.texasalmanac.com/places/perezville",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The San Diego Cemetery lies northeast of Perezville, marking one of the town’s oldest burial sites.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        24
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.texasalmanac.com/places/perezville",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "In the eighteenth century, travelers between Goliad and Mier used springs that feed San Diego Creek as a watering hole within the town limits.",
